<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">LLM usage</h2>

<div id="Ax1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used an LLM-based assistant only for writing support, including sentence paraphrasing,
grammar/typo checking, and outline/flow refinement.
The LLM was not used to design experiments, analyze data, or
generate results; all technical content (methods, equations, figures, and code) was
produced and verified by the authors. LLM-suggested text was reviewed and
edited for accuracy, and references were inserted and checked manually.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Partial information decomposition and its estimation</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Introduction of partial information decomposition</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classical mutual information (MI) quantifies the information a single
variable provides about another: <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y)</annotation></semantics></math> measures the reduction in
uncertainty from <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(Y\mid X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(Y\mid X)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shannon, 1948</a>)</cite>.
Extending this analysis to a system with multiple
source variables—for instance, two sources <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with state
spaces <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a target <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> with
state space <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>—is challenging, as the standard
interaction information, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X_{1};X_{2};Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X_{1};X_{2};Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, can be positive or negative.
This sign ambiguity complicates its interpretation and
motivates a decomposition of information into a set of well-behaved, non-negative quantities.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Partial information decomposition (PID), first proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams and Beer (2010)</a></cite>,
addresses this issue. We adopt the definition from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertschinger et al. (2014)</a></cite>,
which decomposes the total
information into three conceptual components: redundancy, uniqueness, and synergy.
These concepts are quantified by four non-negative atoms: redundant information (<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>),
unique information from the first source (<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), unique information
from the second source (<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), and synergistic information (<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">PID postulates the following consistency relations linking the four
atoms to 4 classical mutual information terms:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X_{1},X_{2};Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X_{1},X_{2};Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=R+U_{1}+U_{2}+S," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=R+U_{1}+U_{2}+S,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X_{1};Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X_{1};Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=R+U_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=R+U_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X_{2};Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X_{2};Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=R+U_{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=R+U_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(X_{1};X_{2};Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(X_{1};X_{2};Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=I(X_{1};Y)+I(X_{2};Y)-I(X_{1},X_{2};Y)=R-S." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=I(X_{1};Y)+I(X_{2};Y)-I(X_{1},X_{2};Y)=R-S.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Eqs. <a href="#A1.E7" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>–<a href="#A1.E10" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> establish the algebraic relationship between <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,U_{1},U_{2},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,U_{1},U_{2},S)</annotation></semantics></math> and
the usual information measures,
cleanly separating redundancy (<math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>) from synergy (<math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>) through
the co-information identity Eq. <a href="#A1.E10" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The definition for the PID atoms relies on optimization over the set of <em id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">marginal-matching</em> distributions:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{P}=\bigl\{\,Q\in\Delta:Q(x_{i},y)=P(x_{i},y),\ \forall\,x_{i}\in\mathcal{X}_{i},\ y\in\mathcal{Y},\ i\in\{1,2\}\,\bigr\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{P}=\bigl\{\,Q\in\Delta:Q(x_{i},y)=P(x_{i},y),\ \forall\,x_{i}\in\mathcal{X}_{i},\ y\in\mathcal{Y},\ i\in\{1,2\}\,\bigr\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of all possible joint distributions of <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
This set contains all distributions <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> that preserve the
marginal information of the true distribution <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, while allowing
other dependencies to vary.
Let <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Q}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Q}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denote mutual information under a distribution <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>,
the PID atoms are defined as:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\max_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{1};X_{2};Y)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\max_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{1};X_{2};Y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{1};Y\mid X_{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{1};Y\mid X_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{2};Y\mid X_{1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{2};Y\mid X_{1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=I(X_{1},X_{2};Y)-\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{1},X_{2};Y)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=I(X_{1},X_{2};Y)-\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{1},X_{2};Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, optimizing over <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> isolates each
component of information. For instance,
a distribution <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\star}\in\Delta_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\star}\in\Delta_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes the
total mutual information <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Q}(X_{1},X_{2};Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Q}(X_{1},X_{2};Y)</annotation></semantics></math> does so by reducing the higher-order (synergistic/complementary) dependencies while preserving the source–target marginals.
The resulting gap, used to define synergy in Eq. <a href="#A1.E15" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>,
therefore measures this emergent information.
Similarly, the unique information components (Eqs. <a href="#A1.E13" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and <a href="#A1.E14" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) represent
the minimal necessary conditional information from
each source, while redundancy (Eq. <a href="#A1.E12" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) represents the maximal possible shared co-information.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Under this construction, the four atoms are non-negative and satisfy
the axiomatic relations in Eqs. <a href="#A1.E7" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>–<a href="#A1.E10" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.
A key insight from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertschinger et al. (2014)</a></cite> is that the
optimization problems defining <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="A1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">equivalent</em>, and it is sufficient to solve one of them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>The estimator we leverage: BATCH for continuous representations</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt BATCH, a scalable PID estimator for high-dimensional, continuous input representations <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
large datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>. The method amortizes the optimization problems in Eqs. <a href="#A1.E12" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>–<a href="#A1.E15" title="In A.1 Introduction of partial information decomposition ‣ Appendix A Partial information decomposition and its estimation ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> by
learning a parametric joint <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}(x_{1},x_{2},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}(x_{1},x_{2},y)</annotation></semantics></math> that
lies in the marginal-matching set <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> defined earlier. Specifically, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to approximately solve</p>
<table id="A1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{1},X_{2};Y)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}I_{Q}(X_{1},X_{2};Y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and, by the equivalence of the optimization characterizations,
the remaining PID components are obtained by evaluating
the corresponding quantities under the same <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Neural parameterization and projection.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given mini-batches <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2},\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2},\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>, two encoders <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> output
features that define an <em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unnormalized</em> joint via a similarity matrix:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A\;=\;\exp\!\big(f_{\phi(1)}(\mathbf{X}_{1},y)\,f_{\phi(2)}(\mathbf{X}_{2},y)^{\top}\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A\;=\;\exp\!\big(f_{\phi(1)}(\mathbf{X}_{1},y)\,f_{\phi(2)}(\mathbf{X}_{2},y)^{\top}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A[i][j][y]=\tilde{Q}(\mathbf{X}_{1}[i],\mathbf{X}_{2}[j],y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A[i][j][y]=\tilde{Q}(\mathbf{X}_{1}[i],\mathbf{X}_{2}[j],y)</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To enforce the marginal
constraints <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\!\in\!\Delta_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\!\in\!\Delta_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, BATCH applies the Sinkhorn-Knopp algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cuturi, 2013</a>)</cite> to iteratively normalize rows and columns of <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
so the projected distribution matches the fixed pairwise marginals <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x_{1},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x_{1},y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x_{2},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x_{2},y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training objective.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given matrix <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> representing <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}(x_{1},x_{2},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}(x_{1},x_{2},y)</annotation></semantics></math>, the objective can be written as:</p>
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}\;\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}x_{1},y\sim Q(x_{1},y)\\
x_{2}\sim Q(x_{2}\mid x_{1},y)\end{subarray}}\left[\log\frac{Q(x_{2}\mid x_{1},y)\,Q(x_{1}\mid y)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in Y}Q(x_{2}\mid x_{1},y^{\prime})\,Q(y^{\prime}\mid x_{1})\,Q(x_{1})}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{Q\in\Delta_{P}}\;\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}x_{1},y\sim Q(x_{1},y)\\
x_{2}\sim Q(x_{2}\mid x_{1},y)\end{subarray}}\left[\log\frac{Q(x_{2}\mid x_{1},y)\,Q(x_{1}\mid y)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in Y}Q(x_{2}\mid x_{1},y^{\prime})\,Q(y^{\prime}\mid x_{1})\,Q(x_{1})}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">hence gradient descent is leveraged to optimize this via
updating the parameters of <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimating PID values via learned models.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Upon convergence, we estimate the required information terms under the data distribution <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and the learned <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the consistency relations and the optimization equivalence,
the PID components are obtained as</p>
<table id="A1.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;I_{\tilde{Q}}(Y;X_{1},X_{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;I_{\tilde{Q}}(Y;X_{1},X_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{1}\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{1}\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;I_{\tilde{Q}}(Y;X_{1},X_{2})-I_{P}(Y;X_{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;I_{\tilde{Q}}(Y;X_{1},X_{2})-I_{P}(Y;X_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{2}\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{2}\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;I_{\tilde{Q}}(Y;X_{1},X_{2})-I_{P}(Y;X_{1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;I_{\tilde{Q}}(Y;X_{1},X_{2})-I_{P}(Y;X_{1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;I_{P}(Y;X_{1},X_{2})-I_{\tilde{Q}}(Y;X_{1},X_{2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;I_{P}(Y;X_{1},X_{2})-I_{\tilde{Q}}(Y;X_{1},X_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These quantities satisfy the PID consistency equations by
construction and recover the optimization-defined
components when <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is learned.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rationale for adopting the BATCH estimator.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The BATCH estimator provides a practical and scalable method
for calculating these PID atoms from data. The core of this approach is to use neural
networks to parameterize and learn an approximate joint distribution, <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, that satisfies
the required marginal-matching constraints. By optimizing an information-theoretic objective
over mini-batches, the estimator can be effectively applied to large datasets.
We chose to adapt this estimator for two primary reasons. First, it was explicitly
designed for general multimodal learning contexts. Second, and most importantly,
it operates on high-dimensional, continuous features. This latter property
makes it uniquely suited for analyzing modern LVLMs,
as our framework can apply it directly to the rich vector embeddings
these models produce to quantify their internal information dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Ablation study</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our methodology, we examine sensitivity to two implementation choices:
(i) feature summarization (mean pooling, last-hidden state, and max pooling) and (ii) the
confidence threshold <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\!\in\!\{0.2,0.3,0.4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\!\in\!\{0.2,0.3,0.4\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We evaluate four representative LVLMs chosen to span <em id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">families</em>, <em id="A2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scales</em>, and <em id="A2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strategy types</em>:
Qwen2.5-VL-7B and Qwen2.5-VL-72B (fusion-centric, two scales of the same family),
Gemma3-4B (language-centric), and Cambrian-34B (language-centric with more parameters).
Ablations are run on the synergy-driven MMBench and the knowledge-driven PMC-VQA datasets.
Because <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are the primary components in these regimes, we report two summary
tables: synergy <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> on MMBench (Table <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix B Ablation study ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and
language uniqueness <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on PMC-VQA (Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Ablation study ‣ A Comprehensive Information-Decomposition Analysis of Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
